      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "PURPOSE",
          "content": "This case demonstrates the use of carotid stents in off-label emergency condition where standard self-expandable stents doesn't fit to native artery and balloon-expandable stents could not be also recommended."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "CASE REPORT",
          "content": "56 years old patient, EVAR therapy performed 2 months before, suffering acute severe abdominal pain; emergency angioCT showed oclusion of superior mesenteric artery (SMA) and no complications related to previous EVAR. A percutaneuos supraselective trombolysis was performed. After 24 hours control DSA showed partial recanalization and tight stenoses in distal SMA with poor run-off to terminal branches. A 6-8 x 40 mm Carotid Stent (Acculink®, Abbot Vascular) was implanted with good inmediate technical result. After 6 months follow up both SMA and branches remain patent and no further treatment was required."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "tapered self expandable uncovered stents are a good treatment option to achieve better conformability to SMA,and bare metal stents do not compromise side-branches of this artery."
        }
      ]},
